{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's central mechanism relies on 'verisimilitude' to justify the preference for better-corroborated theories without induction. However, this directly contradicts Popper's own later conclusions. In 'Conjectures and Refutations' and subsequent works, Popper admitted that his formal definition of verisimilitude (based on logical content) failed when applied to false theories, as all false theories have infinite content and cannot be ranked by truth-likeness without assuming an inductive structure of reality. By grounding the defense in a concept Popper himself deemed logically defective and unable to solve the problem of induction, the target fails to demonstrate that the preference is defensible *on Popper's own terms*. It essentially solves the problem by invoking a solution Popper rejected as incoherent.","counterexample":["Popper's admission in the Addenda to 'Conjectures and Refutations' (1972) that his quantitative definition of verisimilitude collapsed because the set of true consequences and false consequences of a false theory are both infinite, making comparison impossible without inductive metrics."],"premise":"Popper explicitly rejected verisimilitude (truth-likeness) as a viable basis for preferring theories because he could not define it in a way that avoided inductive assumptions.","successor_question":"If verisimilitude cannot provide a non-inductive ranking of false theories, what specific non-probabilistic metric does Popper offer that survives his own critique of content-based comparisons?","target":"5e9de594afba8ef14af435e0a77e52ba402ff559e198a1b582ee283761e8e8b8"}]}